[s0l0!-header][h1]Creating Objects[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

Let's head over to our [b]main[/b] and create a new object of our class.
[b][u]MyClass.java[/u][/b][code format="java" codeId="757"]class MyClass {
  public static void main(String[ ] args) {
    [b]Animal dog = new Animal();[/b]
    [b]dog.bark();[/b]
  }
}
[/code]
Now, [b]dog [/b]is an object of type [b]Animal[/b]. Thus we can call its [b]bark[/b]() method, using the name of the object and a [b]dot[/b].
The [b]dot [/b]notation is used to access the object's [b]attributes [/b]and [b]methods[/b].
[note]You have just created your first object![/note]
